# Check every text file out with LF, on every platform.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout gets CRLF, and a test that compares an
# `include_str!` of one file against another compares "\r\n" with "\n" and fails
# for a reason that has nothing to do with what it is asserting. That is exactly
# how `the_quickstart_quotes_the_shipped_agent_example_verbatim` broke `main` on
# Windows for over a day: the marker it searches for contains "\n", the README it
# searched had "\r\n", and the find simply never matched.
#
# There are no Windows-native files here that need CRLF - no .bat, .cmd, .ps1 or
# .sln - so normalising everything costs nothing and closes the class rather than
# the instance. The driver being Windows-native is about the code it drives, not
# about the line endings it is written with.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Recorded cassettes and traces are the test contract. Byte-for-byte is the whole
# point of them, so no end-of-line translation may be applied.
*.trace.jsonl -text
*.cassette.json -text

# Binary, and mangling them would be silent corruption rather than a failing test.
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.svg text eol=lf
